00 11 22 33 44 55 66 77     // C (and some assemblers) use "//" to start an inline comment
88 99 AA BB CC DD EE FF     # some assemblers use "#" to start an inline comment
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00     % many assemblers use "%" to start an inline comment
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00     /* C-style block comments also work */

                            // blank lines are allowed
                            // you can have any number of "bytes" on a line -- be it many ...
09 18 27 36 45 54 63 72 81 90 AF BE CD DC EB FA
00 01 02 03                 //... or few
            04 05 06 07     // you can have as much leading whitespace as you want
08 09 0A 0B 0C 0D 0E 0F     /*** this line has 0A, which will end the input, causing the remaining bytes
                                 -- the rest of the line, and all subsequent lines --
                                 to be truncated from the target's input ***/
aa bb CC DD eE fF Aa Bb     // upper- or lowercase hex digits will work


F0 E0 D0 C0 B0 A0 90 80     // The first "byte" of the file is the first byte inputted to the target,
70 60 50 40 30 20 10 00     // consequently, it will be at the start of the buffer. The subsequent bytes
0F 1E 2D 3C 4B 5A 69 78     // will be at correspondingly higher addresses. For example, the ninth byte
87 96 A5 B4 C3 D2 E1 F0     // will be at buffer+0x8, and the seventeenth byte will be at buffer+0x10.
